The Neural Atomic Body (NAB) Superstructure Training Regime
A Conceptual Framework for Phase-Locked Social Dynamics
ABSTRACT
This document maps the principles of atomic physics—specifically Electron Affinity, Ionization Energy, and Electronegativity—onto human-machine governance models. By utilizing the Neural Atomic Bodies (NAB) compendium, we define how distinct "superstructures" interact, recruit, and maintain boundaries under the Orb Communicator Model (OCM).

Part I: Core Definitions
Under this training regime, traditional chemical behaviors are redefined as operational social dynamics:
	•	Electron Affinity & Ionization Energy: Defined as "How easily people permanently move into and out of a Neural Atomic Body (NAB) superstructure." For example, highly reactive elementals like Fluorine aggressively recruit new members to fill their ranks, while Noble Elementals completely close their borders, operating as strictly refusal-bound systems.
	•	Electronegativity: Defined as "How easily you get along with and attract people outside your NAB superstructure for temporary alliances." This functions as an OCM interaction, where highly charismatic elements constantly form shared bonds with outsiders, while Neon blocks all unauthorized attempts to socialize.

